{
  "dom_id": "step-q335834",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14212914/assets/837da5064b1f09449bf10caa6738dbe276d71e922a5a4fa96566010fb8ae320a.png)\n\nConsider the diagram shown above, where $\\overset{\\to }{AE}=a,$ $\\overset{\\to }{DB}=b,$ $\\overset{\\to }{CB}=c,$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}=d.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{EC}$ in terms of  $a,b,c,$ and $d.$ To denote the corresponding vectors, use $a$, $b,$ $c,$ and $d.$\n\n$\\overset{\\to }{EC}=$   \n {{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "We want to find a \"path\" from $E$ to $C$ consisting only of the vectors $a,b,c,$ and $d.$\n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition,\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{EC}=\\overset{\\to }{EA}+\\overset{\\to }{AD}+\\overset{\\to }{DB}+\\overset{\\to }{BC}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14212914/assets/a8b76bf9fc26742d8ba05c962e59986e9f7b596f0b02c8e71c4ead4b45cf542a.png)\n\n \n\nNow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{EA}=-a,\\,\\overset{\\to }{AD}=d,\\,\\overset{\\to }{DB}=b,\\,\\overset{\\to }{BC}=-c.\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14212914/assets/fe663b4bfacc4ef2078e014af664e99123cb803fd26d03a449bf9bf6198629cc.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{EC} & =\\overset{\\to }{EA}+\\overset{\\to }{AD}+\\overset{\\to }{DB}+\\overset{\\to }{BC} \\\\  & =-a+d+b+(-c) \\\\  & =-a+d+b-c.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [
